<title>GERMANY: Most Germans want EMU start postponed - survey.</title>
<headline>Most Germans want EMU start postponed - survey.</headline>
<dateline>BONN 1997-02-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>Seventy-seven percent of Germans want Europe's planned economic and monetary union (EMU) to be postponed from its 1999 start date, a survey released on Sunday said.</p>
<p>The poll for Monday's edition of the weekly news magazine Der Spigel found only 18 percent of respondents favoured introducing the single currency on time.</p>
<p>The survey was carried out for Spiegel by polling firm Emnid, who interviewed 1,500 people between Februay 14 and 17.</p>
<p>The survey was the latest in a series of polls to show increasing German anxiety about the planned single currency, the euro. Some polls have also indicated, however, that the euro ranks fairly low on the list of Germans' pressing concerns.</p>
</text>
